Subject: [PATCH] imx219: J106 carrier support — shared reset GPIO + lower MIPI rate

Two changes for the Auvidea J106 carrier with 6× IMX219 cameras on Jetson TX2.

1) Shared reset GPIO (imx219.c)

On the J106 all 6 IMX219 CSI cameras share a SINGLE physical reset line
(TEGRA_MAIN_GPIO(R,5) = sysfs gpio 461), released at boot by a gpio-hog. The
stock tegracam imx219 driver assumes a PRIVATE per-sensor reset: it
gpio_request()s the line EXCLUSIVELY, frees it on power_put, and drives it LOW
on every power_on/power_off. With one shared line that breaks two ways:

  1. sensors 2..6 fail probe with -EBUSY/-16 (the line is already owned);
  2. opening/closing/crashing ONE sensor resets ALL the others mid-stream.

Fix: treat -EBUSY as success, never free the shared line, never drive it low.
Mirrors the Auvidea TX1 R24.2.1 BSP (release once, never assert).

2) Lower MIPI data rate (imx219_mode_tbls.h)

NVIDIA's stock PLL values produce 912 Mbps/lane MIPI serial rate with zero
margin over pixel data throughput. On the J106's CSI traces this is marginal for
the TX2 NVCSI receiver — captures work after boot then wedge with CIL CRC/short
errors (INTR_STATUS 0x0e0000cd) on a per-boot lottery.

Fix: reduce PLL_VT_MPY 0x39→0x2B and PLL_OP_MPY 0x72→0x55 across all modes,
giving 680 Mbps/lane (−25%). Matches the Auvidea TX1 production BSP exactly.
Soak-tested: 200/200 frames across 4 cameras, zero errors.

Target: L4T R32.7.6 (kernel 4.9.337).
Apply from the kernel source root (the dir that contains kernel/nvidia):
    patch -p1 < patches/0001-imx219-share-reset-gpio-j106.patch

--- a/kernel/nvidia/drivers/media/i2c/imx219.c
+++ b/kernel/nvidia/drivers/media/i2c/imx219.c
@@ -314,12 +314,10 @@
 		return err;
 	}

-	if (pw->reset_gpio) {
-		if (gpio_cansleep(pw->reset_gpio))
-			gpio_set_value_cansleep(pw->reset_gpio, 0);
-		else
-			gpio_set_value(pw->reset_gpio, 0);
-	}
+	/* J106: the reset line is SHARED by all 6 IMX219 and is released once at
+	 * boot by a gpio-hog. Do NOT assert it low here; pulsing reset on a
+	 * power_on would reset every other (possibly streaming) sensor. Mirrors
+	 * the Auvidea TX1 R24.2.1 driver ("release once, never assert"). */

 	if (unlikely(!(pw->avdd || pw->iovdd || pw->dvdd)))
 		goto skip_power_seqn;
@@ -390,12 +388,8 @@
 			return err;
 		}
 	} else {
-		if (pw->reset_gpio) {
-			if (gpio_cansleep(pw->reset_gpio))
-				gpio_set_value_cansleep(pw->reset_gpio, 0);
-			else
-				gpio_set_value(pw->reset_gpio, 0);
-		}
+		/* J106: shared reset - never assert low on power_off, otherwise
+		 * stopping/closing one sensor would reset all the others. (TX1-style.) */

 		usleep_range(10, 10);

@@ -433,8 +427,9 @@
 	pw->avdd = NULL;
 	pw->iovdd = NULL;

-	if (likely(pw->reset_gpio))
-		gpio_free(pw->reset_gpio);
+	/* J106: reset line is shared by all 6 sensors - never free it here,
+	 * otherwise unbinding one sensor would release reset for the others
+	 * (or free a descriptor this instance never owned). */

 	return 0;
 }
@@ -489,9 +484,14 @@
 		goto done;
 	}

-	/* Reset or ENABLE GPIO */
+	/* Reset or ENABLE GPIO.
+	 * Auvidea J106: all 6 IMX219 share ONE physical reset line, so the
+	 * gpio may already be owned by a sibling sensor or by a gpio-hog
+	 * (-EBUSY). Treat that as success and just drive the shared line. */
 	pw->reset_gpio = pdata->reset_gpio;
 	err = gpio_request(pw->reset_gpio, "cam_reset_gpio");
+	if (err == -EBUSY)
+		err = 0;
 	if (err < 0) {
 		dev_err(dev, "%s: unable to request reset_gpio (%d)\n",
 			__func__, err);
--- a/kernel/nvidia/drivers/media/i2c/imx219_mode_tbls.h
+++ b/kernel/nvidia/drivers/media/i2c/imx219_mode_tbls.h
